WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.734
Ordinary JavaScript running on one Node.js

2
00:00:02.734 --> 00:00:05.155
event loop executes synchronous sections
sequentially.

3
00:00:05.155 --> 00:00:07.389
That does not make an HTTP

4
00:00:07.389 --> 00:00:10.368
request, asynchronous function, or
business operation indivisible.

5
00:00:10.368 --> 00:00:14.092
An Express service can have many requests
waiting for database

6
00:00:14.092 --> 00:00:18.189
results while timers, callbacks, and
message handlers continue making progress.

7
00:00:18.189 --> 00:00:21.261
An await boundary suspends one
continuation; it does

8
00:00:21.261 --> 00:00:24.426
not reserve the state that continuation
previously observed.

9
00:00:24.426 --> 00:00:27.033
Another request can change an account
between

10
00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:29.826
loading its balance and attempting a
debit.

11
00:00:29.826 --> 00:00:31.874
Await is not inherently unsafe.

12
00:00:31.874 --> 00:00:34.854
The defect is the assumption that a
condition

13
00:00:34.854 --> 00:00:38.391
remains true while separate operations act
on shared state.

14
00:00:38.391 --> 00:00:40.439
Concurrency also crosses runtime
boundaries.

15
00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:42.953
Other Node.js processes, containers,
serverless invocations,

16
00:00:42.953 --> 00:00:45.374
queue consumers, administrative tools, and
external

17
00:00:45.374 --> 00:00:47.794
services can modify the same records.

18
00:00:47.794 --> 00:00:50.029
Worker threads can execute JavaScript in

19
00:00:50.029 --> 00:00:52.449
parallel and may share memory explicitly.

20
00:00:52.449 --> 00:00:55.894
Asynchronous interleaving does not require
simultaneous CPU execution, while

21
00:00:55.894 --> 00:00:59.432
parallel execution adds another way for
operations to overlap.

22
00:00:59.432 --> 00:01:01.759
Begin with an invariant: a condition

23
00:01:01.759 --> 00:01:04.552
that every permitted state transition must
preserve.

24
00:01:04.552 --> 00:01:06.600
Inventory must not become negative.

25
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.766
A coupon may have only one successful
redemption.

26
00:01:09.766 --> 00:01:13.303
An approval may leave the pending state
only once.

27
00:01:13.303 --> 00:01:16.376
A withdrawal must satisfy its balance
constraint, and

28
00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:19.913
an edit must not silently erase another
writer's change.

29
00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:22.520
Naming the invariant makes the correctness
requirement

30
00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.313
more precise than simply requesting a
lock.

31
00:01:25.313 --> 00:01:28.851
Then locate the authoritative state and
enumerate its writers.

32
00:01:28.851 --> 00:01:31.365
Lost updates, duplicate execution,
oversubscription, stale

33
00:01:31.365 --> 00:01:33.320
decisions, and conflicting transitions are

34
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.740
different failures that require different
controls.

35
00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.068
TypeScript can model states and results,

36
00:01:38.068 --> 00:01:40.861
but types do not coordinate runtime
operations.

37
00:01:40.861 --> 00:01:43.933
Promise.all can observe concurrently
started work; it does

38
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:47.098
not turn that work into an atomic
operation.

39
00:01:47.098 --> 00:01:50.450
If correctness depends on a condition
remaining true while

40
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.522
shared state changes, the check and
protected transition

41
00:01:53.522 --> 00:01:57.060
must meet at the layer that owns that
state.

